// The TECHNICAL decision for this frame — how the record is encoded, what executes against
// it, and what a human types. It sits beside discovery because a decision belongs to the
// problem that forced it: discovery says what the system must DO, and this says what it is
// BUILT FROM.
//
// It carries no method content. What a frame is, what a slice is, which phases exist, which
// invariants hold — all of that is the frame, the storm, the capabilities and the notional
// architecture. A decision that legislates the method is a second writer for facts the
// record already owns, which is how ADR.260819.02 grew a schema and thirteen amendments in
// one day before being withdrawn.

adr "ADR.260819.01" {
    slug "kdl-state-and-the-rust-engine"
    title "KDL state, a compiled Rust engine, and the CLI surface"
    state "accepted"
    decided-at "2026-08-19"
    matured-at "2026-08-20"
    deciders "human:waelrabadi" "agent:principal-engineer" "agent:product-designer" "agent:product-manager"
    raised-by frame="FRAME.260819.01"

    approval "acceptance" {
        status "signed"
        signer "human:waelrabadi"
        at "2026-08-19T00:00:00-05:00"
        note "transcribed from the sealed Markdown record, which carries Status: Accepted and \
            names its deciders. Not a signature given at port time"
    }

    body {
        section "problem" #"""
            Praxis holds its own operational state in semi-structured Markdown — frontmatter,
            tables, checklist markers — and five failure modes follow from using a narrative
            format as a state machine:

            1. Fragile in-place regex mutation. Agents mutate tables and frontmatter by
               search-and-replace, which corrupts syntax, jitters whitespace and drops content.
            2. Multi-document state drift. Status duplicated across the dashboard, the sprint,
               its ledger and the initiative drifts silently, because nothing mechanically
               synchronises them.
            3. Silent scope-dropping at close. Deleting an unmet criterion makes a close
               succeed, and no tool objects.
            4. A shell portability and performance ceiling. Roughly fifteen check scripts
               calling jq, sed, grep and inline python spawn hundreds of subshells, differ
               between BSD and GNU userlands, and cannot express invariants like DAG cycle
               detection or bidirectional rollups.
            5. Domain mismatch in quality probes. The standard verify path assumes a web
               service — HTTP observability, externalised config, stateless request paths —
               and produces irrelevant warnings on compilers, CLIs and libraries.

            This is the substrate half of FRAME.260819.01's ceiling. The frame's own words:
            the obstacle is the substrate, not the doctrine.
            """#

        section "evidence" #"""
            The Standoff (SSF compiler) repository was audited as a working precedent for
            mechanical state: a slice ledger with a closed vocabulary of failure shapes, and a
            gap register enforced bidirectionally — the close fails if a gap is mentioned in
            prose without an id, or if a resolved gap is still open. Load-bearing pointers
            eliminated silent scope-dropping outright rather than making it easier to detect.

            On the runtime: a single compiled binary loads a hundred state files, validates
            schemas, checks DAG dependencies and evaluates ratchets in memory in single-digit
            milliseconds, with no host dependency on python, jq or a particular bash.
            """#

        section "decision" #"""
            A KDL-first state architecture, executed by a compiled Rust CLI, with every
            published document a whole-document projection of that state.

            1. State is KDL. All transactional state is held as KDL nodes. Markdown is never a
               mutable state store, and — per this decision's own maturation — never a source
               inside the state root at all.
            2. Whole-document synthesis, zero regex. Rendering compiles fresh whole documents
               from state. Tooling never search-and-replaces over an existing document. This is
               where the regex ceiling actually dies: a spliced document can be corrupted, a
               regenerated one cannot.
            3. Functional core, imperative shell. A pure core holds domain types, codecs,
               invariant validators and templates; a shell holds subcommands, process execution
               and diagnostics. The core is where invariants are decidable; the shell is where
               the world is untidy.
            4. Domain profiles. The repository declares its profile and the check path
               activates only the guardrails that apply, which is what stops service probes
               producing false positives on a compiler or a library.
            """#

        section "layout-and-concurrency" #"""
            Absorbed from the withdrawn ADR.260819.02, where it was the one section that was a
            technical decision rather than method. The state root is a git-backed filesystem
            mutated by many agents at once, so the layout is a concurrency design, not a
            taxonomy. Five rules produce the property:

            1. One file per independently-mutated entity — never a registry file. A single
               registry serialises every agent that touches it.
            2. References are stored on the side the acting agent already owns, so an agent
               writes only in its own subtree.
            3. Nothing derived is stored. A rollup file is a file every agent must write, which
               is the worst contention shape available. Single-writer ownership and lock-free
               concurrency turn out to be the same design.
            4. Ids are allocable without coordination.
            5. Immutable entities are write-once: frozen contracts, accepted decisions and
               released releases have zero contention by construction.

            One safety rule is absolute: never rely on git's textual merge for state files. A
            three-way merge of two KDL edits can produce a syntactically valid document with a
            semantically wrong graph, which is worse than a conflict because nothing announces
            it. Files are single-writer or add-only with distinct names; where neither holds, a
            conflict is raised and resolved by re-running the mutation, never by merging hunks.

            Where two agents would collide on one file, the collision is left in place on
            purpose: under the disjointness rule that work was never legitimately parallel, so
            the layout makes a disjointness violation surface as a merge conflict. The conflict
            is the signal, not a defect to engineer away.
            """#

        section "the-stack" #"""
            Matured 2026-08-20, from named-in-passing to decided.

            Language and edition: Rust, 2024 edition. The toolchain is the current stable
            release, pinned in rust-toolchain.toml so every agent and every CI run compile with
            the same compiler — an unpinned toolchain reintroduces the BSD-versus-GNU class of
            defect this decision exists to remove, one layer up.

            Crate topology: praxis-core (pure) and praxis-cli (shell), one workspace.

            CLI: clap 4, derive API. The derive form keeps the command surface a type rather
            than a builder script, so the shell's argument model is checked by the same
            compiler as the core's domain model.

            Diagnostics: miette. A checker whose output cannot point at the offending line and
            span is a checker people route around, and this one exists to be believed.

            Encoding: KDL v2 specifically, not KDL generally. The spike already depends on v2 —
            #true/#false keywords and the multi-line string dedent rule — so a v1-only parser
            cannot read the tree that exists today.
            """#

        section "alternatives" #"""
            Kept because a solution held without its siblings was inherited rather than chosen.

            JSON / JSONC — strictest parsing and the most reliable to generate, with no
            ergonomic multi-line prose. Rejected: the record carries paragraphs, and escaping
            them into string literals makes the source unreadable in review, which the
            trust-transfer thesis cannot afford.

            YAML — good block scalars, weak type system, frequent indentation drift under
            generation. Rejected: a state machine whose parse depends on whitespace an agent
            emits is the current failure mode in a new syntax.

            CUE — strict, with a genuine constraint lattice. Rejected: high generation error
            rate on its advanced types, and the constraint power lands in the wrong place —
            invariants belong in the engine where they can carry a diagnostic.

            Embedded relational store — strongest query story. Rejected: unreviewable in a pull
            request, and the method depends on a human reading reasoning in review.

            KDL — node-and-attribute AST, no indentation traps, raw multi-line strings that
            carry prose without escaping, slashdash node commenting. Selected.

            Shell scripts retained — rejected on the portability and expressiveness ceiling
            recorded in `problem`, which is the frame's baseline, not a preference.
            """#
    }

    amendment "A1" raised-by="FRAME.260819.01" {
        change "ADR.260819.02 is withdrawn, and its supersession of this decision with it. This decision is accepted in full, not in part"
        change "its layout and concurrency rules are absorbed here, as the only part of it that was a technical decision"
        why "its subject matter — what a frame is, what a slice is, which phases exist, which invariants hold — belongs to the frame, the storm, the capabilities and the notional architecture, all of which now carry it directly. What remained was a schema, and a schema that needs thirteen amendments in a day is not a decision"
        note "this also dissolves the partial-supersession problem: with .02 gone there is nothing to be partially superseded by, and the adr state vocabulary no longer has to express it"
    }

    amendment "A2" raised-by="FRAME.260819.01" {
        change "no Markdown is a source inside the state root. Prose that the record owns is KDL; every Markdown file in the repository is generated"
        why "carried forward from the withdrawn decision, where it amended a commitment that put prose in Markdown fragments. The distinction between source and projection is the one this decision exists to make unmistakable, and a state root holding two kinds of source blurs it"
        note "currently violated by the two walk records, which are Markdown — see `open`"
    }

    amendment "A3" raised-by="FRAME.260819.01" {
        change "the stack is decided rather than named in passing: Rust 2024 edition on a pinned stable toolchain, clap 4 derive, miette diagnostics, KDL v2"
        why "every one of these was a passing mention in the original record, and the spike has since depended on KDL v2 specifics. A dependency the decision does not state is a dependency nobody can review"
    }

    open "which KDL crate parses v2, and whether knuffel can" \
        note="the original named knuffel and serde. knuffel targets KDL v1, and this tree is v2 \
            by the spike's own grammar. Either a v2-capable parser is selected or the encoding \
            claim is not implementable as written. Decide before praxis-core has a codec"

    open "the exact stable toolchain version to pin" \
        note="stated here as policy — current stable, pinned in rust-toolchain.toml. The literal \
            version is written at the first commit that needs it, by whoever runs the toolchain, \
            because a version invented in a document is a fact nobody verified"

    open "where the method's normative schema lives, now that it is not here" \
        note="the withdrawn decision carried the entity catalog, trail vocabulary, approval \
            kinds, placement rules, thirty-five invariant ids and the fitness function, and \
            config.kdl bound to it so a checker could read vocabularies from a decision rather \
            than hardcode them. Under `an ADR is a technical decision` that binding has no \
            target. The schema is not lost — it is parked — but it has no home in the tree"

    trail {
        entry at="2026-08-19T00:00:00-05:00" by="human:waelrabadi" action="created"
        entry at="2026-08-20T00:00:00-05:00" by="agent:principal-engineer" action="ported-to-graph" \
            note="ported from the sealed Markdown record"
        entry at="2026-08-20T00:00:00-05:00" by="agent:principal-engineer" action="amended" \
            note="moved beside discovery under the frame that raised it; absorbed the technical \
                residue of the withdrawn ADR.260819.02; matured the stack from mentions to \
                decisions; recovered the rejected alternatives, which no record in the tree \
                carried"
    }
}
